# giveuser permission : setfacl -m u:john:rw file
# group permission: setfacl -m g:devops:rwx file
# view acl: getfacl file
# remove user acl: setfacl -x u:john file
# remove group acl :setfacl -x g:devops file
# remove allextended acl: setfacl -b file
# set acl mask: setfacl -m m:rwx file

Shared DevOps Project Directory Using ACL:-
<> ravi
john
kumar

<> /opt/devops-project

Requirements:
User	Access
ravi	Read + Write + Execute
john	Read + Write
kumar	Read only

Step 1 — Create the project directory:-
sudo mkdir -p /opt/devops-project
ls -ld /opt/devops-project
Step 2 — Create a project file:-
sudo touch /opt/devops-project/app.txt
echo "DevOps application configuration" | sudo tee /opt/devops-project/app.txt
cat /opt/devops-project/app.txt
DevOps application configuration

Step 3 — Create users
If john and kumar don't exist:--
sudo useradd john
sudo useradd kumar
id john
id kumar
Step 4 — Give normal ownership to Ravi
For this practice, we'll make Ravi the owner:-
sudo chown ravi:ravi /opt/devops-project
sudo chown ravi:ravi /opt/devops-project/app.txt
ls -l /opt/devops-project
-rw-r--r-- 1 ravi ravi app.txt

Step 5 — Give John access using ACL:-

john → read + write
sudo setfacl -m u:john:rw /opt/devops-project/app.txt
setfacl
   ↓
ACL command

-m
   ↓
modify ACL

u:john
   ↓
user named john

rw
   ↓
read + write

app.txt
   ↓
file
Step 6 — Give Kumar read-only access:-

sudo setfacl -m u:kumar:r /opt/devops-project/app.txt
john  → rw
kumar → r

Step 7 — Verify the ACL:-

getfacl /opt/devops-project/app.txt


# file: app.txt
# owner: ravi
# group: ravi
user::rw-
user:john:rw-
user:kumar:r--
group::r--
mask::rw-
other::r--
user:john:rw-
user:kumar:r--

user:john:rw-
user:kumar:r--
Step 8 — Test John's access
Switch to John:
su - john
cat /opt/devops-project/app.txt
John should be able to read the file.
Now try:
echo "John updated the application" >> /opt/devops-project/app.txt
John should be able to write because we gave him:
rw
Check:
cat /opt/devops-project/app.txt
You should see:
DevOps application configuration
John updated the application
Exit John:
exit
Step 9 — Test Kumar's access
Switch to Kumar:
su - kumar
Read the file:
cat /opt/devops-project/app.txt
Kumar should be able to read it.
Now try:
echo "Kumar changed the file" >> /opt/devops-project/app.txt
This should fail with a permission error because Kumar has only:
r--
Exit:
exit
Step 10 — Test Ravi
Ravi is the owner.
Switch to Ravi:
su - ravi
Check:
cat /opt/devops-project/app.txt
Ravi can read/write according to the normal owner permissions.
Step 11 — Remove John's ACL
Suppose John no longer works on the project.
Remove his ACL:
sudo setfacl -x u:john /opt/devops-project/app.txt
Notice:
Don't write rw here.
Correct:
setfacl -x u:john file
Then verify:
getfacl /opt/devops-project/app.txt
The line:
user:john:rw-
should be gone.
Step 12 — Remove all ACL entries
If you want to remove the additional ACL entries:
sudo setfacl -b /opt/devops-project/app.txt
Then:
getfacl /opt/devops-project/app.txt
Now the named user entries should be gone.
The complete project flow
You can practice this as a sequence:
# 1. Create directory
sudo mkdir -p /opt/devops-project

# 2. Create file
sudo touch /opt/devops-project/app.txt

# 3. Add content
echo "DevOps application configuration" | sudo tee /opt/devops-project/app.txt

# 4. Create users
sudo useradd john
sudo useradd kumar

# 5. Give Ravi ownership
sudo chown ravi:ravi /opt/devops-project
sudo chown ravi:ravi /opt/devops-project/app.txt

# 6. Give John read/write
sudo setfacl -m u:john:rw /opt/devops-project/app.txt

# 7. Give Kumar read-only
sudo setfacl -m u:kumar:r /opt/devops-project/app.txt

# 8. Check ACL
getfacl /opt/devops-project/app.txt

# 9. Remove John's ACL later
sudo setfacl -x u:john /opt/devops-project/app.txt

# 10. Remove all ACL entries
sudo setfacl -b /opt/devops-project/app.txt
